#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf225a9f-68f4-5c7b-87f3-dfde75d31e24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308633} {:answer/id #uuid "dd622a14-745d-5bc4-82e9-e678a61adfea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-2{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308634} {:answer/id #uuid "7a20d413-26f0-53ee-bc1a-c4257a359af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+{x}^{2}-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308635} {:answer/id #uuid "2d19cfcc-2113-534f-87de-bc729ec24926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308636} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf58d12-f56b-5546-befb-36d9d93221da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d19cfcc-2113-534f-87de-bc729ec24926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308636} :answer-field/id #uuid "42c0e438-2e2c-5a8e-abc3-67f8997c9f4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21fc98d5-a07a-53e9-b823-65887e0c4938" :question/math-academy-id "q-332552" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-x-1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$"}]}
